          }
        ],
        "text": "According to League of United Latin American Citizens (Appellants), according to League of United Latin American Citizens (Appellants), in 2003 the Texas Legislature adopted a new congressional district map that increased the number of districts likely to elect Republicans from 20 to 22, moving roughly eight million people and abandoning compactness standards."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "League of United Latin American Citizens (Appellants)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6963a134bbb7706ff77e955582242cd0655b70a90f294afd64a8af0b959c1b26",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6963a134bbb7-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 5,
              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to League of United Latin American Citizens (Appellants), according to League of United Latin American Citizens (Appellants), the plan eliminated one of the four African\u2011American opportunity districts and altered a Hispanic opportunity district, replacing it with a sprawling \u201cland\u2011bridge\u201d district (District\u202f25) that links Latino populations in Austin and McAllen."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Appellants (Nina Perales) and State (Ms. Perales)",
        "citations": [
